WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.670
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:10.460
I apologize for my voice today, I am trapped
in this frail human body, and sometimes it

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:11.460
falters.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.500
But, the papers must go on.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.000
This is one of those papers where I find that
more time I spend with it, the more I realize

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.900
how amazing it is.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.030
It starts out with an interesting little value
proposition that in and of itself, would likely

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.300
not make it to a paper.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.400
So, what is this paper about?

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.899
Well, as you see here, this one is about cubification
of 3D geometry.

00:00:35.899 --> 00:00:41.729
In other words, we take an input shape, and
it stylizes it to look more like a cube.

00:00:41.729 --> 00:00:46.839
Okay, that’s cute, especially given that
there are many-many ways to do this, and it’s

00:00:46.839 --> 00:00:52.289
hard to immediately put into words what a
good end result would be, you can see a comparison

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:54.319
to previous works here.

00:00:54.319 --> 00:00:58.639
These previous works did not seem to preserve
a lot of fine details, but if you look at

00:00:58.639 --> 00:01:02.159
this new one, you see that this one does that
really well.

00:01:02.159 --> 00:01:03.499
Very nice indeed.

00:01:03.499 --> 00:01:08.720
But still…when I read this paper, at this
point, I was thinking…I’d like a little

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:09.720
more.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.470
Well, I quickly found out that this work has
more up its sleeve.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:15.530
So much more.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:19.190
Let’s talk about 7 of these amazing features.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:24.890
For instance, one, we can control the strength
of the transformation with this lambda parameter,

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:29.800
as you see, the more we increase it, the more
heavy-handed the smushing process is going

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:30.800
to get.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.780
Please remember this part.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:43.050
Two, we can also cubify selectively along
different directions, or, select parts of

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.000
the object that should be cubified differently.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.140
Hmm.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.280
Okay.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:54.940
Three, we can even use it to fix flaws in
the input 3D geometry.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:01.510
Four, this transformation procedure also takes
into consideration the orientations - this

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:05.909
means that we can perform it from different
angles, which gives us a large selection of

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:08.889
possible outputs for the same model.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:14.909
Five, it is fast and works on high-resolution
geometry, and you see different settings for

00:02:14.909 --> 00:02:19.580
the lambda parameter here that is the same
parameter as we have talked about before - the

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.969
strength of the transformation.

00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:30.349
Six, we can also combine many of these features
interactively until a desirable shape is found.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:35.170
Seven is about to come in a moment, but to
appreciate what that is, we have to look at

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.170
…this.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:40.989
To perform what you have seen here so far,
we have to minimize this expression.

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:48.220
This first term says ARAP, as rigid as possible,
which stipulates that whatever we do in terms

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.379
of smushing, it should preserve the fine local
features.

00:02:52.379 --> 00:02:58.329
The second part is called a regularization
term that encourages sparser, more axis-aligned

00:02:58.329 --> 00:03:02.950
solutions so we don’t destroy the entire
model during this process.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:08.560
The stronger this term is, the bigger say
it has in the final results, which, in return,

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:09.940
become more cube-like.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:12.700
So, how do we do that?

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.829
Well, of course, with our trusty little lambda
parameter.

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:21.579
Not only that, but if we look at the appendix,
it tells us that we can generalize this second

00:03:21.579 --> 00:03:25.050
regularization term for many different shapes.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:31.529
So here we are, finally, seven, it doesn’t
even need to be cubification, we can specify

00:03:31.529 --> 00:03:33.590
all kinds of polyhedra.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.730
Look at those gorgeous results.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.189
I love this paper.

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:43.779
It is playful, it is elegant, it has utility,
and, it generalizes well.

00:03:43.779 --> 00:03:48.590
It doesn’t care in the slightest what the
current mainstream ideas are and invites us

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.400
into its own little world.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.370
In summary, this will serve all your cubification
needs, and turns out, it might even fix your

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.439
geometry, and more.

00:03:58.439 --> 00:04:01.090
I would love to see more papers like this.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:06.180
In this series, I try to make people feel
how I feel when I read these papers.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:20.959
I hope I have managed this time, but you be

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:26.660
the judge.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:05:03.490
Let me know

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:06.570
in the comments.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.680
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

